4.3 Type C Interactions: DDL-Compatible Collaboration Through Corpus-Extending Provocation

If Type B represents the downside of AI scaffolding, Type C interactions demonstrate its transformative potential. Across all three phases, participants who experienced high frequencies of Type C “surprise harvest” interactions reported stronger authorship and satisfaction. The mechanism is iterative reciprocity: ownership emerges not from proportional word contribution but from directional control exercised through rejection–refinement cycles. P12’s creative journey in Phase 2 illustrates this architecture with clarity. Despite AI contributing an estimated 15–20% of final words, the participant claimed 90% self-authorship—the highest across all participants. The collaboration felt mutual: the participant controlled emotional architecture and conceptual direction, while the AI supplied linguistic resources and formal realization. Creativity was complementary rather than competitive.

This procedural ownership explains the inverse relationship between AI word contribution and subjective authorship across our phases. When AI contributions arrive as Type C provocations—unexpected metaphors requiring judgments of fit, surprising connections demanding assessments of relevance, novel framings needing decisions about adoption—word contribution can rise to 40–50% while still feeling like collaboration, because the learner exercises directional control at every decision point. Each rejection–refinement cycle becomes a micro-assertion of creative authority, accumulating into robust ownership despite substantial AI involvement.

In contrast, Type B’s passive selection model yields the opposite effect: even 15–20% AI word contribution can feel like creative abdication because learners never exercise generative control—only evaluative selection among predetermined options. The cognitive architecture differs fundamentally: Type C demands synthesis (How do I integrate this unexpected suggestion into my evolving vision?), while Type B requires only selection (Which pre-made option fits adequately?). Synthesis develops creative capacity; selection merely exercises choice among externally generated possibilities.

The critical distinction is therefore not only technical but pedagogical. Educators can harness Type C by designing activities that foreground agency, require critical evaluation, and reward decision-making rather than uptake. This connects directly to self-efficacy. McQuire et al. (2024) show that engagement declines when learners receive an AI-generated poem and are asked to edit it, yet the creativity deficit disappears when they co-create with AI and establish self-efficacy. Our data suggest that Type C interactions create the conditions for self-efficacy because they require learners to make, justify, and revise creative decisions. In other words, co-creation does not merely add AI output; it reorganizes the locus of control. The learner’s sense of authorship is anchored in decisions about direction, not the proportion of text written.

This distinction also reframes the transition from functional writing to creative writing. Functional writing is target-driven and often task-constrained; creative writing depends on intrinsic motivation and imaginative risk. Type C interactions can catalyze that shift by supplying provocative inputs that invite interpretation rather than compliance. The AI becomes a furnishing tool or inspiration, not a replacement, and the learner’s creative motivation becomes the central outcome. Educators can therefore design for Type C by emphasizing exploratory prompts, encouraging rejection and reworking, and making the learner’s rationale visible in reflective talk or written annotations.

Type C interactions also resolve the apparent contradiction between AI assistance and DDL principles by transforming AI from pattern-retriever to pattern-extender. Traditional corpus DDL operates within a corpus-bounded possibility space—discovering patterns that exist in training data, frequencies that reflect what has been written. This is invaluable for developing corpus-typical competence (knowing what educated native writers conventionally do), but it constrains creative exploration to reproducing existing patterns. Type C, enabled through exploratory parameters that sample from broader token distributions, extends beyond corpus frequencies into a generative possibility space. The “random non-related stuff” that P12 valued represents AI’s capacity to suggest connections, techniques, and framings emerging through novel pattern recombination rather than corpus retrieval. These provocations are unlikely to appear in traditional concordance searches because they do not exist as high-frequency patterns—they emerge through creative divergence.

Critically, this extension preserves DDL’s core insight that patterns discovered are better learned than rules transmitted. Type C does not present authoritative answers requiring acceptance; it generates possibilities that demand critical evaluation. Learners must assess relevance, judge fit, evaluate appropriateness, and selectively integrate suggestions—cognitive operations that rely on pattern understanding rather than pattern reproduction. The “hands-on” engagement central to DDL pedagogy persists, shifted from querying static corpora to evaluating dynamic provocations.

This pedagogical framing aligns with UNESCO’s AI Competency Framework for Education (2024), which emphasizes a human-centred mindset: AI must strengthen human capacities, and selection, use, and monitoring of impact must remain human-controlled and accountable. In AI-assisted poetry writing, the human-centred outcome is what learners gain: deeper understanding of poetics, wider expressive techniques, more fluent use of AI as a tool for articulating artistic experience, and stronger ability to discern and evaluate co-created output. Type C interactions make these gains more likely because they require learners to exercise judgment, articulate intention, and negotiate meaning.

In short, the difference between Type B and Type C is not merely technical but instructional. Type B automates pattern discovery by presenting pre-selected exemplars, collapsing exploratory space into curated options and shifting learners from discoverers to selectors. Type C amplifies pattern discovery by generating provocations that extend beyond corpus frequencies, expanding the exploratory space beyond what concordance searches could surface and positioning learners as critical evaluators synthesizing unexpected possibilities into coherent expression. One represents DDL’s death through automation; the other represents its evolution through augmentation. For educators, the task is to design the classroom ecology so that AI functions as collaborator—supporting, prompting, inspiring—while self-efficacy and human agency remain the centre of the creative process.